/*
Problem: Minimum Number of Arrows to Burst Balloons (LC 452)
Topic: Greedy / Interval Scheduling

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Each balloon is represented as an
interval [start, end].

One arrow shot at position x bursts
every balloon whose interval contains
x.

Greedy strategy:

Always shoot the arrow at the ending
point of the earliest-ending balloon.

This maximizes the number of balloons
burst with one arrow.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Sort all balloons by their ending
   coordinates.

2. Shoot the first arrow at the end
   of the first balloon.

3. Traverse the remaining balloons.

4. If:

   currentStart > arrowPosition

   the current arrow cannot burst it.

   Shoot a new arrow.

5. Otherwise:

   The current balloon is already
   burst by the existing arrow.

6. Return the total arrows used.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[10,16],[2,8],[1,6],[7,12]]

Sorted by end:

[1,6]

[2,8]

[7,12]

[10,16]

Arrow 1 at x = 6

Bursts:

[1,6]

[2,8]

Next balloon:

[7,12]

7 > 6

Need Arrow 2.

Answer:

2

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]]

No intervals overlap.

Need one arrow for each balloon.

Answer:

3

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Choosing the earliest ending balloon
leaves the maximum room for future
overlapping balloons.

Any later ending position can only
burst fewer or the same number of
balloons.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n log n)

Sorting dominates.

Traversal is O(n).

Space : O(1)

Ignoring sorting space.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Greedy + Interval Scheduling
*/

class Solution {
public:

    static bool cmp(vector<int> a,
                    vector<int> b) {

        return a[1] < b[1];
    }

    int findMinArrowShots(
        vector<vector<int>>& points) {

        sort(points.begin(),
             points.end(),
             cmp);

        int count = 1;

        // Current arrow position
        vector<int> curr = points[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < points.size(); i++) {

            // No overlap, need another arrow
            if (points[i][0] > curr[1]) {

                count++;
                curr = points[i];
            }

            // Else, current arrow already
            // bursts this balloon
        }

        return count;
    }
};
